joining me now is the shadow ex-checker  secretary to the treasury abina  zari hello to you thanks for joining us  on the program this morning it's great  to see you good morning uh right we're  still waiting to um hear what's  happening as far as sue gray is  concerned we'll come back to that in  just a second windfall tax biggest uh  clue yet from the government this  morning that potentially it's on the  cards  i saw that this morning um i saw simon  clark do his interview with you saying  that the government was um looking into  this  the reality of the situation is that  labour has been calling for this as you  know for quite some months there is a  real urgency in terms of the fact that  the windfall tax needs to be implemented  we're in the middle of a cost of living  crisis  where households are facing the biggest  squeeze  in 30 years in my constituency i'm  having constituents that aren't able to  get the bus to go and get support in  terms of from the food bank or being  able to go to the job centre it's we  need urgency from the government and i'm  just disappointed to hear from simon  clark to say  that they're waiting to see from the gas  companies in terms of um showing  leadership on this you know the  government's empowered now they've been  powerful 12 years i don't understand why  what the diver and delay is you know gas  and all producers have made huge profits  during this time and it's increasing  it's just a no-brainer in terms of the  fact that the government should get on  with it how should it be used okay so  what labour's been proposing is that  with the windfall tax that we're  proposing this money would be used to  help people in terms it will take up to  600 pounds of their energy bills should  it be across the board  so this is tested i have to say though  um you know it doesn't matter whether  you're i know that people that are  middle incomes whether they're not  taking benefits they are also massively  impacted you know things are really  difficult not as much as people who  can't afford to put their meter on and  are using candles to light their rooms  and um  so my question again is should it be  means tested or not i i don't believe it  should be mean tested because everybody  should get it i i'll say that everyone  is is feeling the pinch um i think the  chancellor is i'm not here to talk about  whether the chancellor is i'm talking  about the majority of individuals i've  seen it from speaking to people on the  doorstep we recently had elections  things are really difficult for  individuals but people in multi-million  pound homes should not be getting 600  quid when actually it could more money  could be given to those who need it most  surely i'd say what the government has  done at the moment which is um introduce  um  increase in insurance contribution and  sorry  national insurance contributions um is  not the best time to be doing that  because it's just making people's  situations worse  which is why we feel that a windfall tax  you know would make things a lot better  because it should be targeted to people  who need it most well it will take up to  600 pounds of bills for people that  really need it to be honest  people in the middle there are fees that  feel in the pinch right now  and people and multimedia as you  probably see  with  one of the energy companies yesterday  was talking about how the fact that you  know  come october september bills are going  to increase dramatically you may think  right now that this isn't impacting a  certain amount of people but by  september october if the government  doesn't get a grip on this it will just  make things a lot worse for individuals  talk to me about the sue gray report um  it's uh it's coming up the prime  minister's uh had one fine he's not  getting any more fines he's not going  anywhere  well i think in terms of the sue grey  report i do feel that it's important  that the investigation takes place i  really want to see what the outcome of  this comes out of this i'd say that i'm  going to be putting my constituents at  the forefront of this they've made  really difficult decisions in some cases  some people weren't able to say goodbye  to their loved ones whereas the prime  minister um has you know we've heard  that they've been having parties  one thing that i do want to see from the  prime minister is to show some  leadership on this i don't want my  concern is that you know we're going to  see um some junior staffers having to  take the blame for this you know the  prime minister needs to show some  leadership and really demonstrate that  you know he's learned from his actions  and something needs to be done how would  that manifest itself what do you want to  see  well we need to see what comes up the  sugar report first i don't want to  pre-judge it we don't have to we've seen  the highlights but we haven't seen the  the report in detail no he's going to be  criticized well i know before i before i  make that judgment i want to see what  comes out of the report and i want to  see what i want i want to see he has  been criticized but i want to see what  comes out the recommendations from sue  gray you know i think uh sigre is you  know is a civil servant who is you know  is acted really responsibly and you know  i trust her judgment and i really want  to see what comes out of that first  before we make some kind of judgment on  what should be done following the sue  grey report it's only been fined once so  and it could be that your boss is in the  same position  i'd say that the situation with um with  kirsten is is different where um the  tier system was completely different and  it was a meal at the end of the day but  you know one thing that um kirsten has  done  is that he said you know if he is fined  he will step down from that and i  believe that is much more strong  leadership than what the prime minister  has demonstrated throughout this  whole situation  okay and talk to me though about the  meeting that allegedly took place  between sue gray and the prime minister  actually it's confirmed that the meeting  took place does it actually matter who  called that meeting i mean all we know  at this stage is that a meeting has  taken place  sue grace says that she didn't call this  meeting we haven't really heard from the  prime minister whether he called the  meeting or not and that to me  is quite concerning to be honest and in  terms of the fact that there is a lack  of transparency if it's not a big deal  why doesn't the prime minister say well  who called the meeting  but at this stage you know we do need  answers on that but we also need to see  what comes out from the sue grey report  does it concern labor that there was a  meeting at all actually given that we we  all agree that sue gray has a massive  integrity  why does it matter whether a meeting  took place or not well to be honest i'm  not saying that it matters whether  meeting took place or not i don't know  what happened within that meeting so  it's very difficult for me to comment  but what i do find quite baffling is  that we can't get any answers as to who  called the meeting  which you know does come across to me as  quite concerning to be honest on that we  don't know what's been discussed in that  meeting  until we find that those details it's  very difficult to make a judgment as to  you know why this meeting took place or  whether it was appropriate  good to talk to you thanks for joining  us on the programme this morning just  bear with us a second if you were  pleased thank you

